# The worst line is the first one: `expect_err ... : ()`. The stop fence's probe
# is best-effort — it swallows a failed query and returns Ok — so sourcing
# liveness from the recovery inventory made it FAIL OPEN. A live manager
# terminal, an unreadable board, and the fence permits the stop. The update
# fence fails the other way: it propagates the inventory's own I/O failure in
# place of a liveness verdict.
exit: 101

# Stop fence: a live manager run went unrefused because the inventory could not
# be read. `()` is the Ok payload the fence returned instead of the refusal.
contains: a live manager run must still be refused when history is unreadable: ()

# Update fence: the refusal became someone else's I/O error.
contains: refusal must name the live run, got: daemon returned 500 Internal Server Error

# The recorded path is the structural proof, and pins the direction: the fence
# asked the inventory. A rewrite that keeps the assertions but stops recording
# paths cannot quietly pass this signature.
contains: the fence must ask the live source and never the recovery inventory
contains: left: ["/api/runs"]
contains: right: ["/api/runs/live"]

